 "comment": "Unicode and whitespace in the narration, and what a citation's label keeps. Payments of Wages from Bank A. The narration is upper-cased whole before it is read: t01 (written with the sharp s, U+00DF) and t02 (STRASSE) are one payee, as are t03 (the long s, U+017F) and t04 (SIGMA), t05 (the dotless i, U+0131) and t06 (DIL), t07 (the fi ligature, U+FB01) and t08 (FINE). Not read, in the first UPI layout: t09 (the Kelvin sign U+212A in place of K), t10 (an accented letter), t11 (a Devanagari name) and t12 (full-width letters). t13 to t17 name OMEGA MART with other whitespace between the words: a no-break space, U+2028, U+001C, U+0085, and in t17 a leading no-break space and an ideographic space among ordinary ones. Not read: t18 (a zero-width space inside the name), t19 (a leading U+FEFF) and t20 (U+2010 in place of the hyphens). t21 (Devanagari digits) and t22 (full-width digits) name PI STORES by IMPS; t23 (a superscript two after a digit) and t24 (a circled digit) are not read. t25 has Devanagari digits and a space in the reference of the second UPI layout and names RHO. In that layout t26's name field is a Devanagari word, a hyphen and the letters KA, read as one name; t27 (Devanagari only) and t28 (Greek only) are not read. t29 writes TAU with a combining circumflex, t30 and t31 with the composed letter (t31 in lower case): two payee rows, one of 2819 paise and one of 5949, whose names the dump shows alike. t32 is a cheque whose name starts with an accented letter: not read. t33 (in the name of the first UPI layout), t34 (in the NEFT code) and t35 (in an IMPS name) put a Devanagari digit where only 0 to 9 are read: not read. t40 to t43 name NU: t40's label carries its voucher type name, Bank Payment; t41 has an empty number (two spaces before on in its label) and t42 a number of two spaces (four spaces); t43's type and number are written with combining accents, which the dump shows composed. Eleven payees; the fifteen payments not read are in unresolved_bank_total (32655 paise) and the recipient_not_read finding.",
